00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.080
Jupiter fabric is
a great keyword.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:58.290
So in terms of how do you store
the data for render jobs, yeah,

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:01.170
there's all sorts of
different offerings at Google.

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:03.900
It's all object
and block for us,

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:06.240
and so we'll just dig into
that a little bit more.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.532
On the object side, we
have Google Cloud Storage.

00:05:08.532 --> 00:05:09.990
I'll talk about
the different tiers

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:12.480
of that, and the availability
of them and how they work

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:13.980
and durability and such.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:17.520
But on the block side,
that's really more analogous

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.380
to what you're used to
dealing with on prem.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:23.160
So everybody is using an NFS
posits compliant file system

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.620
on prem, that's just
what the tools need.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:29.100
And in that context, we
offer persistent disk,

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.347
both HDD for hard
drive variant, which

00:05:31.347 --> 00:05:33.180
is our standard persistent
disk, and we also

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.490
offer SSD, persistent disk.

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:39.480
Now, these PD offerings are
both network attached storage,

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:43.980
and they are incredibly fast.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.690
What's cool about them is
they can scale as well,

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:49.750
so expect these numbers
to go up over time.

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:52.350
The other cool thing
that I love is local SSD,

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:54.090
and we'll go into
some use cases later,

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:59.610
but that is super high speed
attached to the VM physically.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:05.210
It's 700,000 IOPS, basically
the speed of DDR ram, DR2

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:08.130
in terms of use
capabilities, and it's

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:10.440
less expensive than
buying RAM in the machine.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:13.290
So you may want
to have an option

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:17.610
to control the size of your VM
and augment it with local SSD.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:20.070
We can talk about those
strategies in a bit.

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:24.270
In terms of the block store
for Google Cloud Storage,

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:26.100
multi-regional--

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:27.750
fantastic offering
in the fact that it

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:30.210
lets you store data
in two locations that

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:32.290
are more than 150 miles apart.

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:33.750
So if you're
serving content, you

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:35.520
want to have disaster
recovery, you

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:38.970
want to guarantee that you have
the data in multiple locations

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:42.840
that are physically far
apart and disparate,

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.180
then multi-regional
is your friend.

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.940
For most VFX workloads
for storing the data,

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.350
we recommend regional.

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:51.930
That guarantees the
data's in the region

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:53.460
you're doing the compute on it.

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:56.040
It's inexpensive,
highly available.

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:59.760
We also have near line and cold
line as longer term storage

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:01.680
options.

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:03.330
So the neat part
with all this is

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:05.910
that it all runs in
the same subsystem,

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:09.060
and the same API for access.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.370
You have the ability
to get all the data

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:15.360
available to you on a per object
basis within milliseconds.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:18.595
There's no difference in the SLA
for access time between this.

00:07:18.595 --> 00:07:20.970
You can see the pricing, this
is all available on the web

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:22.479
as well.

00:07:22.479 --> 00:07:24.270
The-- I guess the kind
of relevant use bits

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:26.640
are regional and
multi-regional, you're

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.770
just paying for what
use as you use it.

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:31.360
Near line has a 30 day,
and cold line has a 60--

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:33.060
sorry, 90, I should say.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:36.640
The use case that we see is
people aging the data off.

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:38.730
So you set it on
regional and use it,

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:41.580
and you can, if you choose, set
an auto timer that will move it

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:44.880
to the longer term tiers,
or you can do so yourself,

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:46.890
as I mentioned, on
a project basis.

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:48.670
With near line, that is--

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:50.420
that's data we tend to put for--

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:51.510
kind of about a year.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:53.301
If you're going to
access it within a year,

00:07:53.301 --> 00:07:54.977
near line makes the
best economic sense.

00:07:54.977 --> 00:07:57.060
If you're going to leave
it parked for more than a

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:59.700
year and it's kind of
your LTO tape replacement

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:01.740
as a long term
storage option, we

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:03.547
encourage people to
put it on a cold line,

00:08:03.547 --> 00:08:05.130
and that gives you
the option of being

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:07.819
able to park the data there
for a very long period of time.

00:08:07.819 --> 00:08:09.360
I had a question in
my talk yesterday

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:12.100
about bit rot, what
do we do about that.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:13.800
We have some
proprietary technology

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.810
that goes through and
ensures the data's fresh

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:17.400
and ensures the integrity of it.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.280
So you can park your data on a
cold line with the confidence

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:22.029
that it's going to be
there years from now

00:08:22.029 --> 00:08:23.636
when you go to read it.

00:08:23.636 --> 00:08:24.760
So using them all together.

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:27.400
As I mentioned, the per object
storage class is really cool.

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:29.860
If you put 15
petabyes on and you're

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:32.320
working with 300 terabytes
that may be large,

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.299
and someone comes to
you and it's a VFX show,

00:08:34.299 --> 00:08:36.173
and you're working on
a sequel, because there

00:08:36.173 --> 00:08:37.990
are so many of those
these days, and you

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:40.440
need to go back and get the
geometry and texture files.

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:41.510
You're going to
rerig the character,

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:43.360
you're going to put the
different muscle set up on it

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:45.130
and such, but you want
the texture files.

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:47.650
You want the MARI
archive, you want

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:49.600
to get the geometry
that was used.

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:52.120
You can go through and just
pull those objects out,

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:54.267
and you can get access to
them incredibly quickly.

00:08:54.267 --> 00:08:56.850
If it's something you're going
to be rereading multiple times,

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:57.610
move it to regional.

00:08:57.610 --> 00:09:00.110
If you're just going to read
it once and restore it locally,

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:03.000
then just grab it
on a cold line.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:06.067
So in terms of NFS
file system options,

00:09:06.067 --> 00:09:07.650
there's a couple of
different options.

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:10.200
We have a roll your own single
node filer, which we think

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:11.320
is pretty great.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:13.590
It's inexpensive, and the
fact that it's open source,

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:15.090
so it's essentially
free, but you're

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:17.550
paying for the VM
and the storage

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:20.160
is attached to it and the
network that it runs on.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:21.960
But there's no additional
cost beyond that.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:24.660
It is highly performant
because of our network,

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:27.180
and the local SSD is
[INAUDIBLE] cache,

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:28.740
and PDS is [INAUDIBLE] it.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:31.320
So we find that we can
typically serve line rate

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:32.820
to a significant
number of machines.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:36.150
You can also build out
multiple single load filers.

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:39.150
An example would be, you have
a kangaroo and a robot fight

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:41.406
sequence, and you want to
put the kangaroo on one

00:09:41.406 --> 00:09:42.530
and the robot on the other.

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:44.238
So you put the alembic
data on those two.

00:09:44.238 --> 00:09:45.970
You can put the
textures across those.

00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:48.300
You can put a right
node elsewhere,

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:51.510
or you can split alembic caches
on one and textures on another.

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:54.180
If you have six different
characters in some superhero

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:56.340
film, you can split them
out across multiples,

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:58.440
and that's a fairly
common strategy.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:00.742
That's fine if you want
to manage your nodes

00:10:00.742 --> 00:10:02.700
and if that's how you're
architectured on prem.

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:05.190
There's also Gluster, which
is available as a managed

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:06.420
open source solution.

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:07.950
By managed, I should
say supported,

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:12.120
so a supported or a open
source self-supported solution.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:13.855
Both of those are
cloud file systems,

00:10:13.855 --> 00:10:16.230
and the fact that you have to
synchronize the data to get

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:18.230
there, we'll talk about
that an asset management

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:19.230
a couple of minutes.

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:21.180
In terms of caching
file systems,

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:26.100
though, we have a vFXT
and also Pixcache.

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:28.110
The cool part with those
is they're a cloud file

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:30.420
system that are NFS
pauses compliant,

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:32.670
but they also have a read
through cache functionality.

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:36.480
So you export your on premises,
high performance file system

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:38.850
to the cloud, and then
as your render nodes

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:41.790
read additional data, they hit
the cloud file system first.

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:44.520
If it's not there, they go
back on prem, grab it, and then

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:46.410
pull it up, and then
you're able to read it

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:49.360
multiple times in the cloud.

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:53.100
So this is a fantastic
diagram made by Adrian Graham,

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:55.600
a Solutions Architect in LA.

00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:00.270
He put this together, and
it explains pretty much how

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:02.070
you'd architect for the cloud.

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:04.200
This is something he
and I collaborated on.

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:06.540
In terms of-- maybe
you have license server

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:09.420
on prem and cloud
based license server.

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:13.417
There's a high speed
accelerated UDP transfer.

00:11:13.417 --> 00:11:16.000
We can talk a little bit about
how to get things to the cloud.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:17.950
The neat thing
about this, though,

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:20.422
is if you remove
the license server

00:11:20.422 --> 00:11:22.380
and leave everything else
up, pretty much as it

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:25.740
is, you can go through and
take the rendering VMs over

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:27.940
in the corner and
changes those to GPUs.

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:29.951
Put an oil and gas
workload on this,

00:11:29.951 --> 00:11:31.950
and suddenly you have a
high performance compute

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:33.840
scenario for that workload.

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:36.900
You can either choose to
go with local SSDs attached

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:39.270
to the machines for
simulation workloads,

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:43.320
or you can have a large NFS
file system in the cloud,

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:44.970
and your choice of
readthrough cache

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:48.010
is just dependent on
how your pipeline works.

00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:50.010
I'll wait until evereyone
gets their photograph.

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:52.680
This will be available
on YouTube shortly, so,

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:54.580
excited about that.

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:56.850
But again, for me, the
most exciting thing

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:59.850
is the fact that any of the
different high performance

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:01.744
compute workloads I
mentioned earlier run

00:12:01.744 --> 00:12:02.910
with this same architecture.

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:06.570
We have Genomics and
financial services all running

00:12:06.570 --> 00:12:09.900
on this similar architecture
with really solid success.

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:11.520
Now, once you're
committed to us,

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:14.820
getting the data into the cloud,
that's the important part.

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:16.842
So we offer gsutil,
which is a command line

00:12:16.842 --> 00:12:18.300
tool you have on
your local machine

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:20.640
that you can use to push
data to and from the cloud.

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:22.560
But simpler technologies--
or, actually,

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:25.200
alternate technologies,
such as rsync or Parsync,

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:26.640
with commercial
offerings as well,

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:30.110
with partners for
Aspera and Signiant,

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:33.150
those are for the
capability of providing

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:36.070
significant threaded
upload capabilities.

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:39.030
I'm sure everybody in this
room is making pictures,

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:41.850
and you guys are all
familiar with the technology.

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:44.671
The important thing here
is that Google is open.

00:12:44.671 --> 00:12:46.170
If you want to run
your open source,

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:49.140
if you want to grab Tsunami
and compile it and put it

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:50.996
up and use that as your
transport protocol,

00:12:50.996 --> 00:12:51.870
we're cool with that.

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:53.760
Our transfer mechanism,
I should say.

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:57.050
If you want a commercial managed
service, that's fine too.

00:12:57.050 --> 00:13:00.310
In terms of asset management,
getting the data to the cloud,

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:02.414
that's--

00:13:02.414 --> 00:13:04.940
it's a good problem to have.

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:08.750
The easy model is, OK,
we've done this stereo film,

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:11.309
we finaled the left eye, now
we want to take all the data,

00:13:11.309 --> 00:13:13.850
we know everything that we need,
push it all up to the cloud,

00:13:13.850 --> 00:13:17.000
and then spin the VMs up
and render the second eye.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:18.960
We've had a couple
of customers do that.

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:21.890
That's not a common workload,
it's really easy in the fact

00:13:21.890 --> 00:13:23.540
that, again, you
know everything you

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:25.415
need from a dependency
standpoint in advance,

00:13:25.415 --> 00:13:27.404
you put it up, you
make the pictures.

00:13:27.404 --> 00:13:29.570
More realistically, as a
commercial workflow or film

00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:32.989
workflow, where you're
updating assets on the fly.

00:13:32.989 --> 00:13:35.030
You've got your lighter
making changes in Kitana,

00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:37.594
they're pushing the button,
textures may have changed.

00:13:37.594 --> 00:13:40.010
You're in a scenario where
there's always something that's

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:41.450
changed before the
render goes up,

00:13:41.450 --> 00:13:42.800
you need to make
sure it's there.

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:44.674
So these are a couple
of different strategies

00:13:44.674 --> 00:13:47.500
to be able to get the data up.

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:49.790
The simplest thing we do
from a pipeline standpoint

00:13:49.790 --> 00:13:52.120
is encourage people to
just put a simple rsync

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:53.480
in in their publishing step.

00:13:53.480 --> 00:13:56.030
So when they publish and
commit and version up

00:13:56.030 --> 00:13:58.820
within their pipeline, and then
let the next downstream user

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:00.860
know, or inform Shotgun,
or however you've

00:14:00.860 --> 00:14:03.110
chosen to architect
it, a simpler rsync

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:04.844
will push that data
up to the cloud.

00:14:04.844 --> 00:14:06.260
If you have a
caching file system,

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:09.320
you can have a micro node
sitting on the back side that

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:10.970
will read that data
through the cache,

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:14.760
and it will be warm
at least to be there.

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:16.790
One thing I should
point out on this slide,

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:20.486
we have one customer who I
think does a very clever thing.

00:14:20.486 --> 00:14:21.860
After the render's
complete, they

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:25.140
create an H264 render of that.

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.110
And they do that at a
fairly low resolution.

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:29.300
They don't care about color
accuracy, what they want

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:31.390
is a really low bit rate, but--

00:14:31.390 --> 00:14:32.570
I should clarify.

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:34.364
A reduced bit rate,
but sufficient quality

00:14:34.364 --> 00:14:36.030
to make sure the
render worked properly.

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:38.140
Did the textures fall off, are
the lights in the right place,

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:40.100
does the render look
the way it should, then

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:42.140
they bring down their
2 or 4K, or however

00:14:42.140 --> 00:14:44.000
large it's going
to be, giant EXR

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:45.740
files with 50 AOVs and such.

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:48.740
So that's a way to
manage your egress costs,

00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:51.710
to ensure that you're not
just pulling 100% of the data

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:52.880
that you've written.

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:54.800
That's one thing we
discourage, in the fact

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:57.170
that if you pull every
temp file, every log file,

00:14:57.170 --> 00:14:59.479
every simulation cache
down, your egress costs will

00:14:59.479 --> 00:15:00.770
be higher than they need to be.

00:15:00.770 --> 00:15:02.660
You can leave all that
data sitting in the cloud,

00:15:02.660 --> 00:15:04.660
and it's going appear and
grab the bits you want

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:05.630
and pull them back out.

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:07.254
If you also leave
the data in the cloud

00:15:07.254 --> 00:15:09.440
when you want to run
compositing passes later,

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:11.630
that's a really nice idea,
because you've already

00:15:11.630 --> 00:15:12.890
got it there.

00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:14.780
So as far as scheduling
jobs, we work

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:17.060
with a number of
commercial queuing options,

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:20.300
and bringing your own is
absolutely acceptable.

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:25.220
So in terms of block storage
for rendering, persistent disk,

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:30.440
most customers are using
PD SSD in terms of backing

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:32.480
their filers with that.

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:35.550
As an example, the Avere
will run on PD SSD,

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:37.449
but it will also
run on local SSD,

00:15:37.449 --> 00:15:38.990
and there's a
significant performance

00:15:38.990 --> 00:15:40.790
enhancement when you do that.

00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:42.710
If you run the
Avere on local SSD,

00:15:42.710 --> 00:15:46.490
you get roughly a 2x enhancement
across the board in terms

00:15:46.490 --> 00:15:49.550
of sequential reads,
writes, and random writes,

00:15:49.550 --> 00:15:52.932
but you get a 4 and a half time
improvement on random reads.

00:15:52.932 --> 00:15:54.890
And for VFX effects
workloads, it's pretty much

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:55.970
a random read situation.

00:15:55.970 --> 00:16:00.130
So it's super fast
with local SSD.

00:16:00.130 --> 00:16:03.100
In terms of virtual
machines-- so because we

00:16:03.100 --> 00:16:05.710
don't have an entirely
containerized pipeline running

00:16:05.710 --> 00:16:08.300
on Kubernetes at this
time, for the most part

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:12.820
we're using standard VMs
for important workloads,

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:15.490
such as file systems,
gateways, and such,

00:16:15.490 --> 00:16:19.540
and we're using printable
VMs for render nodes.

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:22.150
Now preemtiple VMs are
significantly discounted.

00:16:22.150 --> 00:16:25.120
They're 80% off,
it's a penny a core.

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:28.510
So a 32 core machine is
32 cents US per hour.

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:31.800
So it's-- I made the
joke it's $3, $4.

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:34.300
In that context, you're
in a scenario where

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:37.990
you can run machines that
are incredibly inexpensive,

00:16:37.990 --> 00:16:40.510
and at the same time you can
run a whole bunch of them.

00:16:40.510 --> 00:16:43.295
And you can turn them on and
off on a per frame basis,

00:16:43.295 --> 00:16:46.830
and we'll talk a
little more about that.

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:49.931
You'll notice as well that
it's the same subsystem you're

00:16:49.931 --> 00:16:50.430
running on.

00:16:50.430 --> 00:16:51.346
There's no difference.

00:16:51.346 --> 00:16:53.790
The VM is identical,
it's just how we bill it

00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:55.770
and how we support it.

00:16:55.770 --> 00:16:59.074
We don't offer live migration
on the parental machines.

00:16:59.074 --> 00:17:00.990
If we need to take some
of them back, we will.

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:03.150
It's excess capacity
that we sell.

00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:06.420
So for workloads that are
important and critical

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:09.960
to the infrastructure, that
would be gateways, a license

00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:13.680
server, file
systems, we encourage

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:15.690
you to be able to keep
those on a standard VM,

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:18.270
and we will migrate those
within our data center

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:21.282
if we have a service requirement
for the rack that they're on.

00:17:21.282 --> 00:17:23.490
We'll move them somewhere
else while they're running.

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:26.250
I think it's really cool to
see a single load filer running

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:28.710
at full load, and then
have it migrate real time

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:31.350
and switch over in an
instantaneous period--

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:33.150
instantaneous, yeah, I know.

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:35.760
It's super fast, and it's
a really cool technology

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:37.190
that only we have.

00:17:37.190 --> 00:17:39.450
The preemptible VMs
we encourage customers

00:17:39.450 --> 00:17:43.080
to run with checkpoints,
snapshots, and such.

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:47.360
So that way you can run an 8,
a 12, a really long render,

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:50.760
and be in a scenario where
if the machine goes away,

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:53.820
you can spin the
machine up and you

00:17:53.820 --> 00:17:55.675
get a 30 second warning
on a preemptible VM

00:17:55.675 --> 00:17:56.550
that it's going away.

00:17:56.550 --> 00:17:58.080
So you can chase
the log on that.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:00.390
You can use our cloud logging
to inform your queuing

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:02.667
system that node is going away.

00:18:02.667 --> 00:18:04.250
When that happens,
you can immediately

00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:06.167
spin up another machine.

00:18:06.167 --> 00:18:08.250
You know, I haven't
personally seen any situations

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:09.041
where we stock out.

00:18:09.041 --> 00:18:11.431
We don't guarantee availability,
obviously, however,

00:18:11.431 --> 00:18:12.930
I personally haven't
seen a scenario

00:18:12.930 --> 00:18:16.140
where a customer has
completely stocked out

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:18.150
a zone in such a way
that they can't fire up

00:18:18.150 --> 00:18:19.380
additional machines.

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:21.090
So in that situation,
that means there's

00:18:21.090 --> 00:18:22.800
a machine on that
rack over there,

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:24.480
and you can spin that one up.

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:26.830
That machine comes up
in about 35 seconds,

00:18:26.830 --> 00:18:29.160
so you can start loading the
scene as that other one is

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:30.826
still writing its
checkpoint, if you can

00:18:30.826 --> 00:18:32.990
get that last checkpoint off.

00:18:32.990 --> 00:18:35.000
So in terms of
different models when

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:38.090
you're looking at VFX
rendering, the most common one

00:18:38.090 --> 00:18:40.400
we run into people who
haven't done any rendering

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:42.890
in the cloud yet is a
rental model where they're

00:18:42.890 --> 00:18:46.240
used to going to Bob and Sarah's
rental service and a truck

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:48.740
rolls in and a couple racks
come often and they rack them up

00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:51.120
and they plug them in and
they bin pack the jobs.

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:55.801
That means they're buying 300
machines for four days or three

00:18:55.801 --> 00:18:56.300
weeks.

00:18:56.300 --> 00:18:58.425
And they wait a couple of
days to get the machines.

00:18:58.425 --> 00:19:00.890
And they have them for a period
of time and back they go.

00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:03.740
Another model beyond that
is a collocation model.

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:05.579
So you don't have
enough space on-prem.

00:19:05.579 --> 00:19:06.620
Perhaps you're in London.

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:08.150
Cooling is an issue.

00:19:08.150 --> 00:19:09.960
Real estate is challenging.

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:11.447
You want artists
there as opposed

00:19:11.447 --> 00:19:12.530
to machines in the closet.

00:19:12.530 --> 00:19:14.690
So you rent a colo
space somewhere else,

00:19:14.690 --> 00:19:15.980
run some fiber to it.

00:19:15.980 --> 00:19:16.880
That's great.

00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:19.100
However, the scale is
fixed in that scenario.

00:19:19.100 --> 00:19:21.890
You've just sort of moved
the problem somewhere else

00:19:21.890 --> 00:19:22.860
in that regard.

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:24.860
The dynamic model is the
one that I'm personally

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:26.526
most excited about
and the fact that you

00:19:26.526 --> 00:19:29.240
take advantage of our per
minute billing capability.

00:19:29.240 --> 00:19:32.450
And you can scale kind of
as much as you need to.

00:19:32.450 --> 00:19:35.690
If you come to us and
say, we need 50,000 cores,

00:19:35.690 --> 00:19:38.120
we don't have just unlimited
cores sitting around

00:19:38.120 --> 00:19:38.720
all the time.

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:41.570
But if you let us know
what it is you need,

00:19:41.570 --> 00:19:43.940
we'll give you a quota that
we can give you access to

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:44.700
at any given time.

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:46.449
But if you need something
really massive--

00:19:46.449 --> 00:19:48.110
you're rendering a
ride film and it's

00:19:48.110 --> 00:19:52.160
a high frame rate or it's
crazy large and it's stereo,

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:54.470
and so now you're
dealing with 6K images

00:19:54.470 --> 00:19:57.579
and there's two of them and
you're doing 60 FPS and such,

00:19:57.579 --> 00:19:59.870
if you need to render that
at final production quality,

00:19:59.870 --> 00:20:01.585
you know in the
schedule from production

00:20:01.585 --> 00:20:03.960
when that's going to occur,
you'll let us know in advance

00:20:03.960 --> 00:20:07.400
and we can get you
120,000 cores or more.

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:09.380
It really just comes
down to working with us

00:20:09.380 --> 00:20:11.960
from a scheduling standpoint,
because Google Cloud

00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:14.240
Platform running on
the same infrastructure

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:17.000
as the rest of Google
is in a scenario where

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:19.790
we can work with the
scheduling and capaciting teams

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:22.280
internally and turn
up and make available

00:20:22.280 --> 00:20:25.370
a large block of machines for
you across a couple zones.

00:20:25.370 --> 00:20:29.670
But at the same time, that's
a huge amount of scale.

00:20:29.670 --> 00:20:33.072
So in terms of workloads, kind
of excited about the fact that

00:20:33.072 --> 00:20:34.530
beyond rendering,
and although this

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:37.500
is a rendering-centric talk,
the same infrastructure that you

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:40.410
put in place architecturally
to be able to run and make

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:43.350
pictures on the cloud enable
you to also do high performance

00:20:43.350 --> 00:20:45.630
geometry caching for really
heavy scenes in Houdini

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:49.710
for instance or in Bifrost
with Maya, simulation, FLIP,

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:52.200
fluid solvers, destruction,
finite element,

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:55.920
deep compositing in Nuke, all
of that's available to you.

00:20:55.920 --> 00:20:59.010
And we're also able to handle
everything from camera ingest

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:01.500
from Camera Raw, debayering,
editorial, all the way

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:05.470
through to archive after you've
done final content delivery.

00:21:05.470 --> 00:21:07.990
So one of the workloads that
I spent a bunch of time tuning

00:21:07.990 --> 00:21:09.530
in production was simulation.

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:12.040
I'm going to talk a little bit
about some fluid simulation

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:14.331
and point out some of the
differences between rendering

00:21:14.331 --> 00:21:17.080
and how to get around some
of the common perceptions

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:18.860
of, how do we do fluid
sim in the cloud?

00:21:18.860 --> 00:21:21.010
That seems really complicated.

00:21:21.010 --> 00:21:22.930
There's a silly amount
of compute and RAM

00:21:22.930 --> 00:21:25.600
required in order to be
able to do simulations.

00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:27.040
The files they
write are massive.

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:28.930
20 to 40 gigs per
frame is what I

00:21:28.930 --> 00:21:34.720
was writing when I was on-prem
at Animal Logic at one point

00:21:34.720 --> 00:21:35.530
for "Sucker Punch."

00:21:35.530 --> 00:21:38.920
I found that almost a
third of my simulation time

00:21:38.920 --> 00:21:40.720
was write speeds
across the network.

00:21:40.720 --> 00:21:43.629
And we had a great network there
and a new high speed filer.

00:21:43.629 --> 00:21:44.920
But just they were large files.

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:45.919
They took time to write.

00:21:45.919 --> 00:21:49.060
And the solver that
I was using initially

00:21:49.060 --> 00:21:51.460
wouldn't initiate
the second frame.

00:21:51.460 --> 00:21:53.440
So we had to make some
tweaks and changes.

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:56.530
But the opportunity to be
able to write to local SSD

00:21:56.530 --> 00:21:57.940
is nearly instantaneous.

00:21:57.940 --> 00:22:01.390
It's super fast with
the high speed storage.

00:22:01.390 --> 00:22:04.770
So the wedging term is something
that a simulation artist

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:06.060
has to do.

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:09.850
It's an old film term moved
forward into digital land.

00:22:09.850 --> 00:22:12.070
But the idea would
be that you want

00:22:12.070 --> 00:22:14.590
to make a fluid
simulation, in this case.

00:22:14.590 --> 00:22:16.570
You have to figure
out what the value is.

00:22:16.570 --> 00:22:18.520
Everybody who's run
simulations in this room

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:20.950
knows that it's a
nonlinear situation

00:22:20.950 --> 00:22:22.135
in terms of the solver.

00:22:22.135 --> 00:22:23.510
In the solver you
can't just say,

00:22:23.510 --> 00:22:25.480
oh, I want to make
the force double,

00:22:25.480 --> 00:22:27.360
so I'm just going
to double the force.

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:28.693
It doesn't always work that way.

00:22:28.693 --> 00:22:31.900
You need time on the box
in terms of number of hours

00:22:31.900 --> 00:22:33.182
of working with the simulator.

00:22:33.182 --> 00:22:35.140
So if I would start a
shot on a Monday morning,

00:22:35.140 --> 00:22:38.507
it might be Wednesday before
I have run enough simulation

00:22:38.507 --> 00:22:41.090
passes to figure out what is the
vorticity going to look like,

00:22:41.090 --> 00:22:42.670
what is the impulse
force look like.

00:22:42.670 --> 00:22:44.900
This is a simple
resolution change.

00:22:44.900 --> 00:22:46.720
And you can see the
number of voxel delta

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:49.900
as well as the change in
the look of the simulation.

00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:52.880
You can notice that the curling
is completely different.

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:54.880
It's not just a higher
resolution version of it.

00:22:54.880 --> 00:22:56.929
The solver actually looks
a little bit different.

00:22:56.929 --> 00:22:58.720
So it's essential for
the simulation artist

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:00.095
to be able to have
an opportunity

00:23:00.095 --> 00:23:03.970
to wedge quickly and be
able to get responses back.

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:06.790
Adrian Graham gave this a go.

00:23:06.790 --> 00:23:10.570
And this is some code
that he put together,

00:23:10.570 --> 00:23:13.720
both a MEL and a Python
version of it for wedging.

00:23:13.720 --> 00:23:15.400
And he used Maya Bifrost.

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:17.530
I've also built this in Houdini.

00:23:17.530 --> 00:23:21.460
It's extensible in your
own solver as well.

00:23:21.460 --> 00:23:24.460
This is how you actually run
the individual simulations.

00:23:24.460 --> 00:23:27.280
The code samples will
be visible on YouTube.

00:23:27.280 --> 00:23:31.720
So you'll be able to reproduce
chunks of this if you wish.

00:23:31.720 --> 00:23:34.020
So in terms of should be
able to deploy the wedges,

00:23:34.020 --> 00:23:36.750
this opportunity is really cool.

00:23:36.750 --> 00:23:39.330
I like the idea of a simulation
artists being able to say,

00:23:39.330 --> 00:23:40.860
I want to run this workload.

00:23:40.860 --> 00:23:44.430
And I want to spin up
eight 64 core machines

00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:46.620
with 250 gigs of RAM.

00:23:46.620 --> 00:23:48.330
You can go with custom shapes.

00:23:48.330 --> 00:23:52.200
So if your solver flattens
out at 48 cores, no problem.

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:54.330
28 cores, whatever
the optimum number

00:23:54.330 --> 00:23:56.760
is in terms of RAM
and CPU, you only

00:23:56.760 --> 00:23:59.430
pay for what you use
on a permanent basis.

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:02.356
So in this context you're going
to do the base simulation.

00:24:02.356 --> 00:24:04.980
And then you're going to have to
mesh that so you have a render

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:05.689
object.

00:24:05.689 --> 00:24:07.230
And then on top of
that, you're going

00:24:07.230 --> 00:24:09.720
to do additional passes
based on that surface

00:24:09.720 --> 00:24:10.770
object you've made.

00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:12.570
And then you end up
with the test render.

00:24:12.570 --> 00:24:14.220
The key though in
this scenario is

00:24:14.220 --> 00:24:16.120
to keep all the
data on the cloud.

00:24:16.120 --> 00:24:18.690
You write the really
expensive base simulation out.

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:19.760
Leave it there.

00:24:19.760 --> 00:24:21.540
The high resolution
mesh you generate,

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:23.050
you can leave it there as well.

00:24:23.050 --> 00:24:24.870
And when you do the
render, you don't

00:24:24.870 --> 00:24:26.730
care about color accuracy
for a simulation.

00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:28.021
You're just looking for motion.

00:24:28.021 --> 00:24:30.870
So you're going to have a
shot camera, and if you wish,

00:24:30.870 --> 00:24:31.590
a witness camera.

00:24:31.590 --> 00:24:34.290
So you can have those
two JPEGs generated.

00:24:34.290 --> 00:24:37.290
You can pull those down, display
them on a simple HTML page

00:24:37.290 --> 00:24:39.930
if you want, and you can see
all eight simulations running.

00:24:39.930 --> 00:24:44.310
And you can choose which
ones you want to kill.

00:24:44.310 --> 00:24:47.590
So in terms of the architecture
of how do you make that work,

00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:49.410
this is a nice
little description

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:51.960
of just saying, we have
some sim VMs in the cloud,

00:24:51.960 --> 00:24:53.580
a license server
to make them work.

00:24:53.580 --> 00:24:56.400
You can point back on-prem, and
then you have some local disk.

00:24:56.400 --> 00:24:57.320
It's really easy.

00:24:57.320 --> 00:24:59.760
You put the files in the
cloud on a local SSD.

00:24:59.760 --> 00:25:00.870
You run the simulations.

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:02.650
When you're done,
you shut it off.

00:25:02.650 --> 00:25:05.027
And as the machines are
running in this example,

00:25:05.027 --> 00:25:07.110
you can turn off three or
four of them right away.

00:25:07.110 --> 00:25:08.651
And you can turn
off additional ones.

00:25:08.651 --> 00:25:10.410
We can get like one,
three, and seven,

00:25:10.410 --> 00:25:12.900
you think those are
going to be a good one,

00:25:12.900 --> 00:25:14.692
you let those continue
to run over lunch

00:25:14.692 --> 00:25:15.900
and come back and check them.

00:25:15.900 --> 00:25:18.270
And then you have some idea
of what kind of numbers

00:25:18.270 --> 00:25:20.370
are going to work for
you to get that look.

00:25:20.370 --> 00:25:22.830
If you want to do a full
workstation build out,

00:25:22.830 --> 00:25:25.140
this is what that
architecture looks like.

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:27.150
And the cool part
with this is you'll

00:25:27.150 --> 00:25:30.490
notice that we have both
local workstations and sim

00:25:30.490 --> 00:25:31.410
VMs working here.

00:25:31.410 --> 00:25:32.970
So you can be in
a scenario where

00:25:32.970 --> 00:25:36.560
you're running both hybrid
and in the cloud for this.

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:40.672
Something with our GPU offering
is we offer both compute GPUs.

00:25:40.672 --> 00:25:43.130
So if you want to use a renderer
like Octane, for instance,

00:25:43.130 --> 00:25:44.796
and you want to be
able to render on GPU

00:25:44.796 --> 00:25:48.060
or you wrote your own custom
GPU fire solver, for instance,

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:51.360
we have a significant
number of compute GPUs that

00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:54.840
are available and becoming
available in all of our data

00:25:54.840 --> 00:25:56.230
centers around the world.

00:25:56.230 --> 00:25:58.650
We also have display
GPUs becoming

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:00.540
available in the
next little bit.

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:02.400
And the cool part
with that is you

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:05.200
can use something like Teradici
for a PC over IP solution

00:26:05.200 --> 00:26:07.230
and you can have a
remote desktop session.

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:10.710
And there are a number of
well-known software providers

00:26:10.710 --> 00:26:14.850
that we're working with who
are in a position of being

00:26:14.850 --> 00:26:17.460
able to offer their software
through this service.

00:26:17.460 --> 00:26:19.560
So the zero client
is really nice.

00:26:19.560 --> 00:26:21.150
You get this tiny little box.

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:24.090
It'll run multiple 2K monitors.

00:26:24.090 --> 00:26:26.760
The nice thing is if you
need to hire 30 artists,

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:28.590
you can go out
and get 30 artists

00:26:28.590 --> 00:26:31.440
and you can give them monitors,
mice, keyboards, Wacom tablets,

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:33.390
and a thin client--

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:34.080
done.

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:36.330
And they're now able
to work in the cloud.

00:26:36.330 --> 00:26:38.580
In a shorter term
scenario, you can

00:26:38.580 --> 00:26:41.940
have artists who can't
get that shot finished.

00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:43.350
They're in a difficult place.

00:26:43.350 --> 00:26:44.740
Their rig is too heavy.

00:26:44.740 --> 00:26:46.980
The muscle sim is
just taking too long.

00:26:46.980 --> 00:26:49.230
You can give them a machine
that has twice as much RAM

00:26:49.230 --> 00:26:51.360
or double the cores or
some combination of that.

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:52.943
And that's really
compelling if you've

00:26:52.943 --> 00:26:56.330
be able to get difficult
shots through the pipeline.

00:26:56.330 --> 00:26:58.796
So a sim factory in the cloud--

00:26:58.796 --> 00:27:00.670
we have a whole bunch
of compute and storage.

00:27:00.670 --> 00:27:02.770
And now we have display
and compute GPUs.

00:27:02.770 --> 00:27:04.510
So we're really
trying to round out

00:27:04.510 --> 00:27:06.610
the complete offering
of what's required

00:27:06.610 --> 00:27:09.640
to be able to have a
completely virtualized pipeline

00:27:09.640 --> 00:27:11.330
in the cloud.

00:27:11.330 --> 00:27:13.330
The last thing that I'm
going to talk about here

00:27:13.330 --> 00:27:17.400
is the idea of a
production safety valve.

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:19.680
It's important, I think,
to be able to engage

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:20.790
with us a little earlier.

00:27:20.790 --> 00:27:23.200
I do get calls from people
and emails saying, hey,

00:27:23.200 --> 00:27:24.900
we're delivering this
thing on Tuesday.

00:27:24.900 --> 00:27:26.500
It's Thursday at
2:00 in the morning.

00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:29.100
And there are some
problems and they

00:27:29.100 --> 00:27:30.690
need to be able to
get something out.

00:27:30.690 --> 00:27:33.480
We can help in that
scenario, it's true.

00:27:33.480 --> 00:27:35.010
But it's quite a
bit easier if you

00:27:35.010 --> 00:27:37.050
engage in a proof of
concept a little earlier.

00:27:37.050 --> 00:27:38.469
And then we're in
a scenario where

00:27:38.469 --> 00:27:40.260
you're set up and able
to run on the cloud.

00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:41.530
You don't have to use it.

00:27:41.530 --> 00:27:42.870
You just have the capability.

00:27:42.870 --> 00:27:44.911
And that way if production
comes to you and says,

00:27:44.911 --> 00:27:46.750
hey, the director
changed their mind--

00:27:46.750 --> 00:27:49.350
that happens-- and we need
to be able to live with this,

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:51.517
or another facility,
schedule doesn't work out,

00:27:51.517 --> 00:27:53.100
we can take on an
extra two sequences,

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:55.160
but we don't have the
render power for it,

00:27:55.160 --> 00:27:56.460
you can say no problem.

00:27:56.460 --> 00:27:57.990
We can render this on Google.

00:27:57.990 --> 00:28:00.090
And we're already
set up to do so.

00:28:00.090 --> 00:28:03.441
And with that, I'd like to
bring Hannes up on stage.

00:28:03.441 --> 00:28:06.447
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:06.447 --> 00:28:07.030
Thank you sir.

00:28:07.030 --> 00:28:07.750
HANNES RICKLEFS: There we go.

00:28:07.750 --> 00:28:08.624
JEFF KEMBER: For you.

00:28:08.624 --> 00:28:10.962
HANNES RICKLEFS:
Brain, thank you.

00:28:10.962 --> 00:28:12.170
And just leave my water here.

00:28:12.170 --> 00:28:12.950
Cool.

00:28:12.950 --> 00:28:15.260
Well, hi everybody.

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:16.760
I'm really super
excited to be here.

00:28:16.760 --> 00:28:18.968
What I'm going to do over
the next kind of 30 minutes

00:28:18.968 --> 00:28:21.930
is to take you on a bit
of a journey for how

00:28:21.930 --> 00:28:24.920
Google Cloud help MPC
kind of bring the "Jungle

00:28:24.920 --> 00:28:27.932
Book" to life, showing
some of the key challenges

00:28:27.932 --> 00:28:29.390
that we faced, give
you an overview

00:28:29.390 --> 00:28:31.740
of the technical design
that we implemented,

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:33.740
and then also give you
kind of an outlook, where

00:28:33.740 --> 00:28:38.060
we kind of see the cloud
going for us in the future.

00:28:38.060 --> 00:28:39.420
First, a little bit about MPC.

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:44.060
So we craft visual experiences
for both feature film

00:28:44.060 --> 00:28:48.470
VFX and advertising, also
including virtual reality

00:28:48.470 --> 00:28:49.790
and digital installations.

00:28:49.790 --> 00:28:53.160
We've been around
for over 25 years.

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:57.680
This is some of the past movies
that we've been working on.

00:28:57.680 --> 00:29:01.670
Here's an overview of all the
projects that we did in 2016.

00:29:01.670 --> 00:29:03.140
Well, actually not all of them.

00:29:03.140 --> 00:29:08.270
We completed a total of 15
projects on the film side.

00:29:08.270 --> 00:29:10.440
And on the advertising
side, it was

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:13.100
2,019, which is a
great number if you

00:29:13.100 --> 00:29:18.750
have to put on the same
slide with 2016 to remember.

00:29:18.750 --> 00:29:23.090
So although we delivered
15 movies in 2016,

00:29:23.090 --> 00:29:25.520
we tend to work on
roughly 20 projects

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:27.690
at any given point in time.

00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:30.890
So these ones are about
to deliver this year.

00:29:30.890 --> 00:29:34.640
But a lot of these were
already in the making in 2016

00:29:34.640 --> 00:29:39.380
and going to be close to screen
to you at some point this year.

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:42.620
MPC is roughly 2 and 1/2
thousand people worldwide,

00:29:42.620 --> 00:29:45.590
the majority being split between
Vancouver, Montreal, London,

00:29:45.590 --> 00:29:47.690
and Bangalore.

00:29:47.690 --> 00:29:50.780
All of our productions are
run across at least two

00:29:50.780 --> 00:29:51.620
of those sites.

00:29:51.620 --> 00:29:54.740
That's predominately due to
make leverage specialisms that

00:29:54.740 --> 00:29:57.090
are only available
in some of our sites.

00:29:57.090 --> 00:29:59.960
So really close collaboration
and data transfers

00:29:59.960 --> 00:30:02.570
between all of these sites
is hugely important for us

00:30:02.570 --> 00:30:04.490
to deliver our productions.

00:30:04.490 --> 00:30:07.220
So talking about
our productions,

00:30:07.220 --> 00:30:09.260
in order for us to create
these visual effects,

00:30:09.260 --> 00:30:11.390
we constantly need
to kind of plan

00:30:11.390 --> 00:30:13.070
how many people,
how much compute,

00:30:13.070 --> 00:30:15.080
and how much storage we require.

00:30:15.080 --> 00:30:16.880
To do that, we look
at each of these shots

00:30:16.880 --> 00:30:18.921
that we can kind of see
and we kind of break them

00:30:18.921 --> 00:30:21.200
down for days that
we think we're

00:30:21.200 --> 00:30:24.020
going to require in each of the
departments that are required

00:30:24.020 --> 00:30:26.510
to make these shots.

00:30:26.510 --> 00:30:28.550
So this gives us the
kind of like total amount

00:30:28.550 --> 00:30:31.040
of mandates that we will
need for the project.

00:30:31.040 --> 00:30:33.140
And from that we derive
schedules, which we then

00:30:33.140 --> 00:30:36.140
use to see kind of that's the
people, that's the storage

00:30:36.140 --> 00:30:37.759
and compute we require.

00:30:37.759 --> 00:30:39.800
I'd kind of like to take
you through that process

00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:41.758
in a bit of detail,
because it's actually quite

00:30:41.758 --> 00:30:42.950
important for how we plan.

00:30:42.950 --> 00:30:44.408
And then you can
kind of see really

00:30:44.408 --> 00:30:48.710
how the cloud allows
us to change that.

00:30:48.710 --> 00:30:51.440
Due to the nature of the
movies that we work on,

00:30:51.440 --> 00:30:53.452
we tend to see these
kind of massive peaks

00:30:53.452 --> 00:30:55.160
throughout the year
in predominantly kind

00:30:55.160 --> 00:30:57.500
of late spring and
late autumn, which

00:30:57.500 --> 00:30:59.990
is due to the summer and
winter blockbuster releases

00:30:59.990 --> 00:31:02.010
that we're working on.

00:31:02.010 --> 00:31:03.942
So as I mentioned, we
do roughly 20 projects.

00:31:03.942 --> 00:31:05.900
What I'm going to do just
for simplicity's sake

00:31:05.900 --> 00:31:08.330
is use an example
of kind of three

00:31:08.330 --> 00:31:10.220
somewhat fictitious projects.

00:31:10.220 --> 00:31:12.470
But the kind of scale that
I mention kind of literally

00:31:12.470 --> 00:31:15.680
applies to all of
them that we work on.

00:31:15.680 --> 00:31:18.959
So here is kind of the
result of us breaking down

00:31:18.959 --> 00:31:20.750
all of the work that's
going to be required

00:31:20.750 --> 00:31:22.820
and then kind of figuring
out how many people we need.

00:31:22.820 --> 00:31:24.403
And this is really
the starting point.

00:31:24.403 --> 00:31:27.470
From this, we then go and
have our internal models

00:31:27.470 --> 00:31:29.876
that we then use to predict
the amount of compute

00:31:29.876 --> 00:31:31.625
and the amount of
storage that we require.

00:31:34.430 --> 00:31:38.094
What is kind of interesting,
that depending of the project--

00:31:38.094 --> 00:31:40.260
so depending if it's a
character heavy shot, if it's

00:31:40.260 --> 00:31:41.760
an effects heaving
shot, environment

00:31:41.760 --> 00:31:44.540
heavy shot, these profiles
kind of change quite a lot.

00:31:44.540 --> 00:31:46.790
So you can see this kind
of on the storage side.

00:31:46.790 --> 00:31:49.100
The big challenge is
that all of these things

00:31:49.100 --> 00:31:53.810
peak at the same time throughout
the production cycle literally.

00:31:53.810 --> 00:31:57.940
So just imagine 15 more of
these kind of stacking up.

00:31:57.940 --> 00:32:00.740
To give you an idea of
scale, when we talk people,

00:32:00.740 --> 00:32:03.620
that's roughly a couple
of hundreds per project.

00:32:03.620 --> 00:32:06.170
On the compute side, we're
talking millions of hours

00:32:06.170 --> 00:32:06.800
to compute.

00:32:06.800 --> 00:32:08.660
And on the storage
side, our projects

00:32:08.660 --> 00:32:11.330
tend to be in around the
petabyte when they kind

00:32:11.330 --> 00:32:14.120
of reach that peak capacity.

00:32:14.120 --> 00:32:16.990
So that means that we
need to manage, plan,

00:32:16.990 --> 00:32:18.680
and provision for these peaks.

00:32:18.680 --> 00:32:21.470
We also need to build all of
our infrastructure to constantly

00:32:21.470 --> 00:32:24.410
kind of be able to scale up
and down for us to deliver

00:32:24.410 --> 00:32:27.900
our productions
without any impact.

00:32:27.900 --> 00:32:30.080
The other thing is when
we look at the trends--

00:32:30.080 --> 00:32:32.030
now this is kind of
like a 12 month schedule

00:32:32.030 --> 00:32:35.420
right here-- we see these
shrinking quite significantly.

00:32:35.420 --> 00:32:38.090
The thing that isn't shrinking
is the complexity of the shot

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:39.479
work that's required.

00:32:39.479 --> 00:32:42.020
So these peaks are just going
to go and increase and increase

00:32:42.020 --> 00:32:44.000
and increase.

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:45.260
Just an idea of scale--

00:32:45.260 --> 00:32:48.290
for the "Jungle Book," we
had 800 people working on it.

00:32:48.290 --> 00:32:52.100
In total it was 35
million wall clock hours

00:32:52.100 --> 00:32:53.030
to compute the movie.

00:32:53.030 --> 00:32:55.640
And at peak, the
"Jungle Book" required

00:32:55.640 --> 00:32:58.880
2 petabytes of storage.

00:32:58.880 --> 00:33:01.400
So let me give you a bit
of background details

00:33:01.400 --> 00:33:03.110
of what the scale and
kind of complexity

00:33:03.110 --> 00:33:04.568
that we faced on
the "Jungle Book."

00:33:04.568 --> 00:33:07.850
In 2016, Disney awarded us
with the majority of the work

00:33:07.850 --> 00:33:10.100
on the "Jungle Book."

00:33:10.100 --> 00:33:14.750
We were tasked with creating
1,200 shots with over hundreds

00:33:14.750 --> 00:33:17.420
of characters that we needed
to build, 60 environments,

00:33:17.420 --> 00:33:19.730
over 2,000 environment assets.

00:33:19.730 --> 00:33:23.630
That resulted in roughly
12 square miles of jungle,

00:33:23.630 --> 00:33:25.392
which according to
Google is a quarter

00:33:25.392 --> 00:33:26.600
of the size of San Francisco.

00:33:26.600 --> 00:33:28.808
So you can kind of get the
perspective of how much we

00:33:28.808 --> 00:33:30.324
actually had to build.

00:33:30.324 --> 00:33:32.240
This watering shot that
you can see right here

00:33:32.240 --> 00:33:34.820
had a total of kind of like
200 characters within it.

00:33:34.820 --> 00:33:36.920
And it was also the
first shot to move up

00:33:36.920 --> 00:33:38.750
to completely to
new software stack.

00:33:38.750 --> 00:33:42.200
Now our software stack uses
a large amount of third party

00:33:42.200 --> 00:33:44.840
software, but then a
huge amount of custom

00:33:44.840 --> 00:33:46.504
tooling that we have to build.

00:33:46.504 --> 00:33:48.170
And this was really
important because we

00:33:48.170 --> 00:33:50.870
needed to hit the creative
and artistic vision that Jon

00:33:50.870 --> 00:33:55.430
Favreau had set out, who was
the director on this movie.

00:33:55.430 --> 00:33:58.490
It's been an absolutely
amazing experience for us,

00:33:58.490 --> 00:34:01.760
really satisfying due to
just the recognition we've

00:34:01.760 --> 00:34:03.170
received through the industry.

00:34:03.170 --> 00:34:04.770
Because it was a
couple of weeks ago,

00:34:04.770 --> 00:34:07.390
I think, that we finally
won the Oscar for the movie.

00:34:07.390 --> 00:34:09.409
So it's been amazing.

00:34:09.409 --> 00:34:12.320
But rather than talking, I
think lights and the audio

00:34:12.320 --> 00:34:14.270
please, because
this is a breakdown

00:34:14.270 --> 00:34:19.173
of some of the FX work that
was required for the movie.

00:34:19.173 --> 00:34:19.839
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:34:19.839 --> 00:34:22.833
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:36:18.528 --> 00:36:19.292
[END PLAYBACK]

00:36:19.292 --> 00:36:20.750
HANNES RICKLEFS:
So as you can see,

00:36:20.750 --> 00:36:22.730
quite a lot of fluid
stimulation, as Jeff was--

00:36:22.730 --> 00:36:23.230
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:23.230 --> 00:36:23.921
Oh, thank you.

00:36:26.570 --> 00:36:27.860
Thank you.

00:36:27.860 --> 00:36:28.605
Right.

00:36:28.605 --> 00:36:30.230
So as I just mentioned
before the clip,

00:36:30.230 --> 00:36:33.210
there was quite a lot
of new technologies

00:36:33.210 --> 00:36:36.110
that was required for
us to deliver the movie.

00:36:36.110 --> 00:36:40.160
One of those particular pieces
of third party technology

00:36:40.160 --> 00:36:42.890
was a new version of Renderman,
which has really moved away

00:36:42.890 --> 00:36:45.400
from its more traditional
rays-based pipeline

00:36:45.400 --> 00:36:48.060
to a completely
native path tracer.

00:36:48.060 --> 00:36:51.080
We have a really great
relationship with Pixar.

00:36:51.080 --> 00:36:52.880
And some of the
initial tests that we

00:36:52.880 --> 00:36:54.590
did with this new
version gave us

00:36:54.590 --> 00:36:57.830
the absolute great confidence
that the photo realistic kind

00:36:57.830 --> 00:37:01.240
of visuals that were
required to deliver

00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:05.320
would be possible
with this new version.

00:37:05.320 --> 00:37:07.280
The thing is, we
fine tune kind of

00:37:07.280 --> 00:37:10.190
like all of our compute tasks
specifically for the task.

00:37:10.190 --> 00:37:12.020
So we look at what's
the optimum in terms

00:37:12.020 --> 00:37:17.240
of CPU versus RAM
versus storage per task.

00:37:17.240 --> 00:37:20.480
In addition, we kind of had to
revamp our internal rendering

00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:23.450
software stack to make
use of this new version.

00:37:23.450 --> 00:37:26.830
So it's actually quite
a significant risk

00:37:26.830 --> 00:37:28.330
for a production
to say, well, we're

00:37:28.330 --> 00:37:30.050
going to change our renderer.

00:37:30.050 --> 00:37:32.090
To give you an idea
of that scale, when

00:37:32.090 --> 00:37:34.760
I mentioned that the "Jungle
Book" took 35 million render

00:37:34.760 --> 00:37:36.380
hours to finish--

00:37:36.380 --> 00:37:39.290
so that includes everything,
like simulations and all that--

00:37:39.290 --> 00:37:42.200
80% of that is
Renderman related hours.

00:37:42.200 --> 00:37:46.100
So it was kind of a significant
risk for us to do it.

00:37:46.100 --> 00:37:48.170
But I'm really glad we did.

00:37:48.170 --> 00:37:50.090
The other thing is,
there was a huge amount

00:37:50.090 --> 00:37:53.150
of internal development
that we needed to do,

00:37:53.150 --> 00:37:55.970
ranging for how do we
model the environments,

00:37:55.970 --> 00:37:58.130
how do we simulate
all the jungle,

00:37:58.130 --> 00:38:00.860
how do we improve our
character animation, and also

00:38:00.860 --> 00:38:02.340
various workflow improvements.

00:38:02.340 --> 00:38:04.700
So one of the ones
that I want to pick on

00:38:04.700 --> 00:38:09.530
is, we really needed to move
up our quality for our QC

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:13.250
and kind of just approval
renderers, which traditionally

00:38:13.250 --> 00:38:17.030
would be more of these kind
of like puppets cut up,

00:38:17.030 --> 00:38:21.710
and tend to use kind of like
OpenGL GPU-based visuals.

00:38:21.710 --> 00:38:23.919
So what we did is
we actually said no,

00:38:23.919 --> 00:38:25.460
we're going to use
Renderman for that

00:38:25.460 --> 00:38:26.960
as well, which meant
that we were going

00:38:26.960 --> 00:38:28.751
to go from something
like this to something

00:38:28.751 --> 00:38:30.410
like this as part
of the QC step.

00:38:30.410 --> 00:38:32.690
It was really important for
our animation department

00:38:32.690 --> 00:38:36.569
to see the impact of fur for of
that kind of little subtleties

00:38:36.569 --> 00:38:37.860
in relation to the performance.

00:38:37.860 --> 00:38:41.480
So this was really important for
us to get right for this movie.

00:38:41.480 --> 00:38:44.300
Now what that meant is that
due to the volume of "Jungle

00:38:44.300 --> 00:38:48.290
Book," due to all of this new
technology, our plans, when

00:38:48.290 --> 00:38:50.330
I showed you those
graphs for compute,

00:38:50.330 --> 00:38:52.340
they reached new heights.

00:38:52.340 --> 00:38:55.370
So in essence we needed to
do and get more compute.

00:38:55.370 --> 00:38:58.130
Now our existing approach, like
Jeff mentioned, we would go out

00:38:58.130 --> 00:38:59.390
and we would go
and purchase and we

00:38:59.390 --> 00:39:00.931
could look at our
data center and get

00:39:00.931 --> 00:39:03.620
all of that stuff in there
and get ready and then use it.

00:39:03.620 --> 00:39:05.030
Or we would go out and lease it.

00:39:05.030 --> 00:39:07.880
But that, for the time
frame that we needed,

00:39:07.880 --> 00:39:11.930
was going to be very cost
ineffective, and also kind

00:39:11.930 --> 00:39:15.350
of challenging to get in
time for when it was needed.

00:39:15.350 --> 00:39:18.830
Now we had already had
multiple conversations

00:39:18.830 --> 00:39:22.190
with Google to see how can we
use the cloud to actually give

00:39:22.190 --> 00:39:23.660
us a third option.

00:39:23.660 --> 00:39:26.210
And this time, like
all parties, said,

00:39:26.210 --> 00:39:27.860
we're going to go to and do it.

00:39:27.860 --> 00:39:29.920
So what did we set out?

00:39:29.920 --> 00:39:34.550
We wanted to add 10,000 cores
for a duration of two months.

00:39:34.550 --> 00:39:36.530
However-- and this is
really important-- it

00:39:36.530 --> 00:39:39.650
was crucial to provide
this additional compute

00:39:39.650 --> 00:39:41.894
with an official
approval from our client.

00:39:41.894 --> 00:39:43.310
So that meant we
needed it to meet

00:39:43.310 --> 00:39:45.890
there's and our internal
security standards

00:39:45.890 --> 00:39:47.900
for cloud-based compute.

00:39:47.900 --> 00:39:49.930
All of our sites are
regularly audited

00:39:49.930 --> 00:39:52.070
literally at multiple
times throughout the year

00:39:52.070 --> 00:39:55.624
on our internal architecture
and our technical implementation

00:39:55.624 --> 00:39:58.040
to ensure that they're aligned
with the security practices

00:39:58.040 --> 00:40:00.020
that are part of the industry.

00:40:00.020 --> 00:40:02.090
To do that, MPC
and Technicolor--

00:40:02.090 --> 00:40:04.550
so Technicolor is
MPC's parent company--

00:40:04.550 --> 00:40:05.990
we partnered with ISE--

00:40:05.990 --> 00:40:07.880
they're the Independent
Security Evaluators--

00:40:07.880 --> 00:40:11.630
to provide a security
assessment of our kind

00:40:11.630 --> 00:40:13.300
of remote compute
platform, and then

00:40:13.300 --> 00:40:16.730
before submitting that proposal
to Disney for approval.

00:40:16.730 --> 00:40:19.430
This was an ongoing process
throughout the whole project

00:40:19.430 --> 00:40:21.770
because any alterations,
any new appliances

00:40:21.770 --> 00:40:24.620
that we wanted to introduce
needed to be re-evaluated

00:40:24.620 --> 00:40:27.210
and then resubmitted
to approval.

00:40:27.210 --> 00:40:30.560
So here is the kind of technical
design that we ended up with.

00:40:30.560 --> 00:40:34.140
MPC's existing infrastructure
is built around centralized NFS

00:40:34.140 --> 00:40:38.300
filers for both software
and production content.

00:40:38.300 --> 00:40:41.764
Both of these receive
multiple updates per day

00:40:41.764 --> 00:40:43.180
in terms of, on
the software side,

00:40:43.180 --> 00:40:44.530
new tools, new configurations.

00:40:44.530 --> 00:40:49.700
So they're in constant kind
of influx and being worked on.

00:40:49.700 --> 00:40:53.650
All of MPC's sites are connected
via Technicolor's production

00:40:53.650 --> 00:40:54.770
WAN.

00:40:54.770 --> 00:40:57.350
But all of these kind of
film production zones,

00:40:57.350 --> 00:40:59.600
which is one of the big
of security requirements,

00:40:59.600 --> 00:41:02.630
do not have any external
connectivity-- so no access

00:41:02.630 --> 00:41:05.670
to the internet from them.

00:41:05.670 --> 00:41:09.190
There are some shared services
zones that you've got here

00:41:09.190 --> 00:41:10.684
that do have external
connectivity.

00:41:10.684 --> 00:41:13.100
But these are tightly controlled
with application and kind

00:41:13.100 --> 00:41:14.749
of like network [INAUDIBLE].

00:41:14.749 --> 00:41:16.790
So the initial design that
we kind of put forward

00:41:16.790 --> 00:41:22.420
is to use VPN tunnels to provide
burst capacity into Google's

00:41:22.420 --> 00:41:24.250
cloud using VPN tunnels.

00:41:27.700 --> 00:41:29.200
The big thing was
is there was going

00:41:29.200 --> 00:41:31.570
to be no production
content in the cloud,

00:41:31.570 --> 00:41:35.500
only for temporary logs and some
scratch base that was required

00:41:35.500 --> 00:41:37.960
for some of our applications.

00:41:37.960 --> 00:41:41.660
To provide these VPN end
points, MPC worked with Sohonet.

00:41:41.660 --> 00:41:45.010
So they have a product
called FastLane

00:41:45.010 --> 00:41:46.810
which enabled us to
get the kind of VPN

00:41:46.810 --> 00:41:49.570
connectivity that we
required given the network

00:41:49.570 --> 00:41:52.570
throughput requirements
that we had.

00:41:52.570 --> 00:41:54.400
So since this
initial setup, we've

00:41:54.400 --> 00:41:56.890
actually started to
use the Google managed

00:41:56.890 --> 00:41:58.930
VPN on the GCP side.

00:41:58.930 --> 00:42:01.630
But we're still on
the FastLane product

00:42:01.630 --> 00:42:04.120
on the Technicolor side.

00:42:04.120 --> 00:42:06.370
With this initial design,
we knew from the beginning

00:42:06.370 --> 00:42:08.286
that we had to deal with
latencies, because we

00:42:08.286 --> 00:42:11.410
weren't going to rearchitect
any of our internals.

00:42:11.410 --> 00:42:16.000
So we chose Europe-West to just
reduce the amount of latency

00:42:16.000 --> 00:42:18.820
that we had from
our London site.

00:42:18.820 --> 00:42:23.290
However, the kind of nine
milliseconds that we measured

00:42:23.290 --> 00:42:25.840
was too much for
some of our tools

00:42:25.840 --> 00:42:28.840
that really were used
to really quick access

00:42:28.840 --> 00:42:30.670
to our application's filers.

00:42:30.670 --> 00:42:35.420
Various different interesting
timeouts were encountered.

00:42:35.420 --> 00:42:38.260
In addition, when we increased
to more and more calls,

00:42:38.260 --> 00:42:40.790
they just became more
and more apparent.

00:42:40.790 --> 00:42:41.790
So to deal with this--

00:42:41.790 --> 00:42:45.250
it was kind of on one of
the slides from Jeff--

00:42:45.250 --> 00:42:47.830
what we initially did is
we put out our application

00:42:47.830 --> 00:42:49.270
file out into the cloud.

00:42:49.270 --> 00:42:51.040
Our preference would
have been to just use

00:42:51.040 --> 00:42:53.680
the Avere virtual
appliance that existed.

00:42:53.680 --> 00:42:58.840
Unfortunately, this didn't
quite work out of the box.

00:42:58.840 --> 00:43:00.430
Because of our
security restrictions,

00:43:00.430 --> 00:43:02.440
what the Avere needs to
do is to actually speak

00:43:02.440 --> 00:43:05.020
to the Google APIs, which
wasn't possible in the setup

00:43:05.020 --> 00:43:07.000
that we had.

00:43:07.000 --> 00:43:08.770
Now we already use
Avere internally.

00:43:08.770 --> 00:43:11.830
We forefront all of our
application and content filers

00:43:11.830 --> 00:43:12.640
with the Avere.

00:43:12.640 --> 00:43:14.560
So we have a great
connection with them.

00:43:14.560 --> 00:43:16.710
They came in and they
actually helped build us

00:43:16.710 --> 00:43:19.780
a custom virtual
appliance that was then

00:43:19.780 --> 00:43:24.190
able to perform within
our system architecture.

00:43:24.190 --> 00:43:25.570
Now along with
the Avere, we also

00:43:25.570 --> 00:43:26.950
had kind of internal
tooling that

00:43:26.950 --> 00:43:29.020
needed to require to
talk to the Google API

00:43:29.020 --> 00:43:31.570
because, hey, we need to
provision machines, right?

00:43:31.570 --> 00:43:32.709
We want new PVMs.

00:43:32.709 --> 00:43:34.000
We want new standard instances.

00:43:34.000 --> 00:43:35.541
So we needed to
figure out a way for,

00:43:35.541 --> 00:43:38.770
how do we make this kind
of communication happen?

00:43:38.770 --> 00:43:40.600
And the proposal
that we put forward

00:43:40.600 --> 00:43:43.030
was to use kind of like
a tiered proxy, which

00:43:43.030 --> 00:43:44.680
kind of enabled
bootstrapping and any

00:43:44.680 --> 00:43:49.120
of the kind of APIs requirements
to get GCP resources

00:43:49.120 --> 00:43:51.670
and for the Avere.

00:43:51.670 --> 00:43:56.170
Now all of this was of course
resubmitted to ISE, and then

00:43:56.170 --> 00:43:59.030
also to Disney for approval.

00:43:59.030 --> 00:44:02.000
So this is kind of the final
design that we put in place.

00:44:02.000 --> 00:44:05.940
So on the GCP side,
we had render nodes.

00:44:05.940 --> 00:44:08.320
So these were dedicated
to run the render task.

00:44:08.320 --> 00:44:11.230
They were a mixture of PVM
and standard instances running

00:44:11.230 --> 00:44:13.990
in MPC custom center six image.

00:44:13.990 --> 00:44:16.900
We had file servers, again,
purely for kind of locks

00:44:16.900 --> 00:44:19.120
and any temporary
scratch that was required

00:44:19.120 --> 00:44:22.780
by some of our application,
also running a custom MPC center

00:44:22.780 --> 00:44:23.530
six image.

00:44:23.530 --> 00:44:27.670
Then the VPN end points, they
were worked on by Sohonet

00:44:27.670 --> 00:44:30.950
to provide us the network
performance through our VPN

00:44:30.950 --> 00:44:33.920
setups, and then the
Avere virtual appliance,

00:44:33.920 --> 00:44:36.490
which was the custom
MPC Avere builds

00:44:36.490 --> 00:44:39.550
to really help on optimizing the
performance that we required.

00:44:39.550 --> 00:44:41.590
And really most
importantly, that no content

00:44:41.590 --> 00:44:44.210
was ever stored at
rest in the cloud.

00:44:44.210 --> 00:44:45.760
We can assume that
behind the scenes

00:44:45.760 --> 00:44:47.980
we had host and key
management all in place.

00:44:47.980 --> 00:44:50.710
And there were strong
role-based authorization

00:44:50.710 --> 00:44:53.560
to ensure strict access for
any kind of admin and support

00:44:53.560 --> 00:44:55.870
throughout this platform.

00:44:55.870 --> 00:44:58.240
Now just-- I like my graphs.

00:44:58.240 --> 00:45:01.600
So here is to show you kind of
the importance of the Avere.

00:45:01.600 --> 00:45:05.140
So this was a graph of
one of the render nodes

00:45:05.140 --> 00:45:06.191
without the Avere.

00:45:06.191 --> 00:45:08.440
And if you kind of pay
attention to the left hand side

00:45:08.440 --> 00:45:11.470
if I change the scale, so once
the Avere was put in place,

00:45:11.470 --> 00:45:15.440
we moved from literally 50 reads
per second up to 2,000 reads

00:45:15.440 --> 00:45:16.330
in a second.

00:45:16.330 --> 00:45:19.150
Now this is really
important because when

00:45:19.150 --> 00:45:22.360
you look at the
performance of a renderer,

00:45:22.360 --> 00:45:23.860
they like to read a lot of data.

00:45:23.860 --> 00:45:25.390
That's due to the
amount of texture,

00:45:25.390 --> 00:45:27.490
to the amount of geometry, and
just various different data

00:45:27.490 --> 00:45:27.990
types.

00:45:27.990 --> 00:45:30.920
So this was really important
for us to be put in place.

00:45:30.920 --> 00:45:32.950
The other thing that
was important was--

00:45:32.950 --> 00:45:36.180
or literally-- as a
consequence, we obviously

00:45:36.180 --> 00:45:39.330
had huge amount of
network traffic drop,

00:45:39.330 --> 00:45:40.810
with the red line
kind of marking

00:45:40.810 --> 00:45:42.490
when we had the Avere in place.

00:45:42.490 --> 00:45:45.340
So at this point in time,
we're really happy, right?

00:45:45.340 --> 00:45:47.200
We solved the security.

00:45:47.200 --> 00:45:49.480
We got all of the throughput
that we needed to do.

00:45:49.480 --> 00:45:51.580
So it was now questions
like, how much can we

00:45:51.580 --> 00:45:53.230
fire up the cloud?

00:45:53.230 --> 00:45:55.840
And this is where I'm going
to hand over to the video

00:45:55.840 --> 00:45:58.300
again to just show
you the choice of what

00:45:58.300 --> 00:45:59.628
we could put up on the cloud.

00:46:02.866 --> 00:46:03.532
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:46:03.532 --> 00:46:06.460
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:46:12.472 --> 00:46:14.180
HANNES RICKLEFS: And
there's audio again.

00:49:45.257 --> 00:49:47.253
[END PLAYBACK]

00:49:47.253 --> 00:49:48.490
HANNES RICKLEFS: All right.

00:49:48.490 --> 00:49:50.318
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:53.520 --> 00:49:55.130
Thank you again.

00:49:55.130 --> 00:49:58.250
Now unfortunately,
our production budgets

00:49:58.250 --> 00:50:00.980
are not quite as
infinite as the resources

00:50:00.980 --> 00:50:02.340
that you can get into the cloud.

00:50:02.340 --> 00:50:04.730
So what we had to do is
kind of a bit of work of,

00:50:04.730 --> 00:50:06.740
how do we identify
what kind of jobs

00:50:06.740 --> 00:50:08.960
makes the most sense
to send to the cloud,

00:50:08.960 --> 00:50:13.464
whilst keeping within our
production constraints?

00:50:13.464 --> 00:50:15.005
First off, we kind
of went into, well

00:50:15.005 --> 00:50:18.680
what's the ideal criteria
to send task to the cloud?

00:50:18.680 --> 00:50:21.249
For us, that was tasks
with limited I/O,

00:50:21.249 --> 00:50:23.540
to just ensure we were not
going to-- because remember,

00:50:23.540 --> 00:50:26.790
the production content
was always on our end.

00:50:26.790 --> 00:50:28.970
So any kind of
download and any rights

00:50:28.970 --> 00:50:31.490
would cost egress charges--

00:50:31.490 --> 00:50:33.530
and generally to be
quite compute heavy.

00:50:33.530 --> 00:50:37.020
So we just make sure we make
use of the great instances

00:50:37.020 --> 00:50:38.570
that we had out there.

00:50:38.570 --> 00:50:40.460
They should be
tolerant to preemption

00:50:40.460 --> 00:50:44.690
so that we could make use of
the lower cost PVMs wherever

00:50:44.690 --> 00:50:46.191
possible.

00:50:46.191 --> 00:50:48.440
In general, that meant that
our lighting and rendering

00:50:48.440 --> 00:50:52.820
tasks just fitted that profile
a lot better than comp and FX,

00:50:52.820 --> 00:50:56.926
which tend to be extremely high
I/O, and also very intolerant

00:50:56.926 --> 00:50:59.550
for preemption if you just kill
the simulation halfway through.

00:50:59.550 --> 00:51:03.420
So our RenderMan tasks were the
ones that we targeted first.

00:51:03.420 --> 00:51:05.740
The improvements
to our QC process

00:51:05.740 --> 00:51:07.400
to move to RenderMan
in comparison

00:51:07.400 --> 00:51:10.940
to kind of the OpenGL renders,
plus the volume of shots

00:51:10.940 --> 00:51:15.100
that we had had thousands of
tasks in our internal backlog.

00:51:15.100 --> 00:51:18.140
So luckily from the QC
kind of perspective,

00:51:18.140 --> 00:51:21.560
you don't necessarily need
a full sequence of frames

00:51:21.560 --> 00:51:23.660
to ensure that things
like lighting directions--

00:51:23.660 --> 00:51:25.535
I mean, I can look at
this image and say yep,

00:51:25.535 --> 00:51:27.050
the lighting direction is OK.

00:51:27.050 --> 00:51:28.630
I don't need to see
everything else.

00:51:28.630 --> 00:51:32.840
We can just move that along
and do a final render.

00:51:32.840 --> 00:51:35.360
And also kind of from
animation perspective,

00:51:35.360 --> 00:51:38.720
you were OK to just see
the performance even

00:51:38.720 --> 00:51:40.110
if kind of frames were missing.

00:51:40.110 --> 00:51:41.660
It was more important
to just have a reference

00:51:41.660 --> 00:51:43.730
point to ensure, yes,
from a QC perspective,

00:51:43.730 --> 00:51:47.160
we're actually able to
move these things along.

00:51:47.160 --> 00:51:49.360
So these kind of QC renders
was a perfect target

00:51:49.360 --> 00:51:52.200
we send to the PVMs.

00:51:52.200 --> 00:51:55.780
Now our production moves through
a large number of departments

00:51:55.780 --> 00:51:57.430
that are kind of shown here.

00:51:57.430 --> 00:51:59.720
All of our work is kind of
split into two main areas.

00:51:59.720 --> 00:52:01.219
We have what we
call the build areas

00:52:01.219 --> 00:52:03.520
where we construct all
of our kind of characters

00:52:03.520 --> 00:52:05.050
and environments.

00:52:05.050 --> 00:52:07.961
And then we reuse those on all
the various different shots.

00:52:07.961 --> 00:52:10.210
For the "Jungle Book," I
mean, every single department

00:52:10.210 --> 00:52:10.810
was important.

00:52:10.810 --> 00:52:13.570
But the kind of two
key ones was animation,

00:52:13.570 --> 00:52:15.220
from just a
performance perspective

00:52:15.220 --> 00:52:17.020
that we had to
deliver, and lighting

00:52:17.020 --> 00:52:19.900
to ensure that we can deliver
the photo realistic visuals

00:52:19.900 --> 00:52:23.920
that were required to meet
the vision of the director.

00:52:23.920 --> 00:52:26.620
Now our ability to add
additional resources

00:52:26.620 --> 00:52:28.810
for the animation QC,
and then from a lighting

00:52:28.810 --> 00:52:32.380
QC and final render
standpoint, enabled production

00:52:32.380 --> 00:52:34.840
to be really confident
that we are going to hit

00:52:34.840 --> 00:52:37.310
the demands of the shows.

00:52:37.310 --> 00:52:39.430
So on a daily basis,
kind of what happened

00:52:39.430 --> 00:52:42.580
was that we looked
at our daily backlog

00:52:42.580 --> 00:52:44.510
in terms of volume
and priorities.

00:52:44.510 --> 00:52:46.930
If required, a budget was set.

00:52:46.930 --> 00:52:50.560
And also priorities
were provided

00:52:50.560 --> 00:52:53.230
to then ensure we knew
kind of like what we wanted

00:52:53.230 --> 00:52:54.760
to get through in the night.

00:52:54.760 --> 00:52:56.740
So we used Tractor,
one of the schedulers

00:52:56.740 --> 00:53:00.220
that was mentioned by Jeff,
for our internal management

00:53:00.220 --> 00:53:03.084
of our hosts and the submission
and processing of the tasks.

00:53:03.084 --> 00:53:04.750
So there was kind of
like two main areas

00:53:04.750 --> 00:53:07.360
that we needed to develop
our own tools for.

00:53:07.360 --> 00:53:09.490
One was for the
automatic provisioning.

00:53:09.490 --> 00:53:12.160
So this was to say given
the budget, how many PVMs

00:53:12.160 --> 00:53:17.170
and how many standard
instances do we require?

00:53:17.170 --> 00:53:18.865
And then on the other
side was kind of

00:53:18.865 --> 00:53:20.350
like how do we
ensure that we can

00:53:20.350 --> 00:53:25.330
get kind of the right tasks
sent over to the cloud?

00:53:25.330 --> 00:53:27.710
What was important
here is in order

00:53:27.710 --> 00:53:30.100
to make best use of
the Avere, we kind of

00:53:30.100 --> 00:53:31.510
needed to ensure
that we can only

00:53:31.510 --> 00:53:35.200
send kind of like the same
kind of scenes or shots

00:53:35.200 --> 00:53:35.860
to the farms.

00:53:35.860 --> 00:53:37.750
So make sure, that,
hey, lets just

00:53:37.750 --> 00:53:40.000
make sure the water hole
shots all go to the cloud,

00:53:40.000 --> 00:53:42.705
or lets make sure that only
the jungle sequence here

00:53:42.705 --> 00:53:43.720
on the bottom goes.

00:53:43.720 --> 00:53:46.600
So that way the cache
wouldn't constantly

00:53:46.600 --> 00:53:48.460
kind of trash itself.

00:53:48.460 --> 00:53:49.840
And that worked really well.

00:53:49.840 --> 00:53:51.760
And then also to say
that Tractor in itself

00:53:51.760 --> 00:53:53.360
really handled the
load quite well

00:53:53.360 --> 00:53:55.180
of all of these new
additional machines

00:53:55.180 --> 00:53:57.602
that we're going to
add on a nightly basis.

00:53:57.602 --> 00:53:59.560
So we didn't need to do
any work to just handle

00:53:59.560 --> 00:54:02.015
the increased load that
was on Tractor itself.

00:54:02.015 --> 00:54:05.506
All right, just
leaves me to conclude.

00:54:05.506 --> 00:54:06.880
First off, here's
some statistics

00:54:06.880 --> 00:54:10.360
of how much we used the cloud.

00:54:10.360 --> 00:54:12.070
At kind of peak,
we added in excess

00:54:12.070 --> 00:54:14.770
of 14,000 cores per night.

00:54:14.770 --> 00:54:19.600
Overall, they computed
360,000 hours.

00:54:19.600 --> 00:54:21.940
That's roughly 41 years.

00:54:21.940 --> 00:54:25.240
1.5 million tasks
were completed.

00:54:25.240 --> 00:54:28.660
Internally, we think this
was a really great success

00:54:28.660 --> 00:54:31.180
across our technology,
operations, and production

00:54:31.180 --> 00:54:33.010
teams because everybody
came together.

00:54:33.010 --> 00:54:35.032
You needed to control
the amount of money

00:54:35.032 --> 00:54:36.490
that we were going
to spend, and we

00:54:36.490 --> 00:54:38.739
needed to know that we
actually can get the throughput

00:54:38.739 --> 00:54:40.570
and have all of the tech ready.

00:54:40.570 --> 00:54:42.550
The agility and just
general availability

00:54:42.550 --> 00:54:45.250
of the cloud resources enabled
literally millions of tasks

00:54:45.250 --> 00:54:47.780
to be completed in
a very short time.

00:54:47.780 --> 00:54:51.050
So if we look at kind of what
I call are the key benefits--

00:54:51.050 --> 00:54:55.390
so the quickly added capacity,
this was really important.

00:54:55.390 --> 00:54:57.920
When a production of
that scale is in demand,

00:54:57.920 --> 00:55:00.430
you want to ensure that they
feel as confident that they can

00:55:00.430 --> 00:55:02.800
that yes, we're going to be
able to deliver the movie.

00:55:02.800 --> 00:55:05.230
This was really
important for us.

00:55:05.230 --> 00:55:09.370
The agility, just in terms of
being able to say, hey, today

00:55:09.370 --> 00:55:13.330
I want 70% PVMs and only
30% standard instances

00:55:13.330 --> 00:55:16.850
was really important so we
can guide it on a daily basis.

00:55:16.850 --> 00:55:18.400
The other thing
that we found, which

00:55:18.400 --> 00:55:21.410
was really cool was that as
I mentioned in the beginning,

00:55:21.410 --> 00:55:24.020
we kind of fine tune our
processes to say, all right,

00:55:24.020 --> 00:55:26.350
what's the split
of CPU versus RAM

00:55:26.350 --> 00:55:28.150
on these particular machines?

00:55:28.150 --> 00:55:30.640
On the cloud, actually
getting a new machine profile

00:55:30.640 --> 00:55:31.374
is really easy.

00:55:31.374 --> 00:55:33.540
You just say, hey, I want
this kind of configuration

00:55:33.540 --> 00:55:33.850
and be done.

00:55:33.850 --> 00:55:35.410
If I would have to
do that internally,

00:55:35.410 --> 00:55:36.868
it's actually quite
a lot of effort

00:55:36.868 --> 00:55:39.664
to make sure that we can
reportion parts of our farm.

00:55:39.664 --> 00:55:41.830
So this actually helped us
to do a lot of playground

00:55:41.830 --> 00:55:47.350
testing The other part is, we
now have established security

00:55:47.350 --> 00:55:49.270
processes and documentation
and everything

00:55:49.270 --> 00:55:51.910
that we can now go and engage
with a lot of our other clients

00:55:51.910 --> 00:55:55.900
to also ensure that we can use
the cloud for their projects.

00:55:55.900 --> 00:55:57.670
Then controlled cost
and [INAUDIBLE],

00:55:57.670 --> 00:56:00.450
again, our budgets
are quite constrained.

00:56:00.450 --> 00:56:02.470
So knowing of what
we can compute,

00:56:02.470 --> 00:56:05.650
what we're able to deliver, and
the associated costs with this

00:56:05.650 --> 00:56:07.820
is really important.

00:56:07.820 --> 00:56:10.120
And then finally, we've
now really established

00:56:10.120 --> 00:56:13.060
some really close partnerships
across the various different

00:56:13.060 --> 00:56:14.330
third party--

00:56:14.330 --> 00:56:17.920
well, the cloud, Jeff
and all these guys,

00:56:17.920 --> 00:56:20.150
which really helps us kind
of look into the future.

00:56:20.150 --> 00:56:22.200
So let's look into the future.

00:56:22.200 --> 00:56:24.160
We strongly believe
in the future

00:56:24.160 --> 00:56:26.050
of the cloud for visual effects.

00:56:26.050 --> 00:56:27.670
So we're now
working on a project

00:56:27.670 --> 00:56:30.970
to literally take our
whole platform cloud

00:56:30.970 --> 00:56:33.490
native to enable us to
process our visual effects.

00:56:33.490 --> 00:56:35.440
Now our core platform
tooling that we already

00:56:35.440 --> 00:56:38.530
have is built around concepts
or cloud-like concepts.

00:56:38.530 --> 00:56:40.870
So we have a strong
microservices framework.

00:56:40.870 --> 00:56:43.750
We have a well established
asset management platform.

00:56:43.750 --> 00:56:46.419
And our software and kind of
build and configuration system

00:56:46.419 --> 00:56:47.710
is all built around containers.

00:56:47.710 --> 00:56:49.370
That we have all
of these things.

00:56:49.370 --> 00:56:50.920
What's nice with
the cloud, I'm now

00:56:50.920 --> 00:56:53.360
able actually run that
in an environment which

00:56:53.360 --> 00:56:55.860
is kind of separated from
our existing infrastructure.

00:56:55.860 --> 00:56:57.830
So I can iterate a
lot quicker, which

00:56:57.830 --> 00:56:59.780
then helps me to build
out that new platform

00:56:59.780 --> 00:57:03.530
and then being able to change
my internal stack a lot easier.

00:57:03.530 --> 00:57:06.350
Also, if this stuff is of
interest to us, we are looking.

00:57:06.350 --> 00:57:07.190
We are hiring.

00:57:07.190 --> 00:57:13.010
So do we have a look at that
link and/or grab me afterwards.

00:57:13.010 --> 00:57:15.040
Now here's kind of the
outlook where we see us

00:57:15.040 --> 00:57:17.096
kind of moving in
terms of the cloud.

00:57:17.096 --> 00:57:19.720
Rather than going through every
single one of those departments

00:57:19.720 --> 00:57:21.550
one by one, what we
actually want to do

00:57:21.550 --> 00:57:24.520
is just take the whole
lot and move to the cloud,

00:57:24.520 --> 00:57:26.560
and then being able to
literally just pull down

00:57:26.560 --> 00:57:29.800
the QC and kind of like final
renders to look at locally.

00:57:29.800 --> 00:57:31.630
Now that would mean
we could use the cloud

00:57:31.630 --> 00:57:33.770
to also manage our
storage constraints

00:57:33.770 --> 00:57:36.670
and more importantly, deal
with the kind of like intersite

00:57:36.670 --> 00:57:39.520
connectivity, because the
global nature of our productions

00:57:39.520 --> 00:57:42.290
just is what we do.

00:57:42.290 --> 00:57:45.290
So this just lets
me to thank Disney

00:57:45.290 --> 00:57:48.170
for just allowing me to show
you not only the amazing visuals

00:57:48.170 --> 00:57:50.990
but also the technical
artistry that was required

00:57:50.990 --> 00:57:53.300
to bring the "Jungle
Book" to life,

00:57:53.300 --> 00:57:56.660
my colleagues at
Technicolor, my team at MPC--

00:57:56.660 --> 00:57:58.490
it's been an ongoing
dedication and passion

00:57:58.490 --> 00:58:01.200
to just embrace this
new technology--

00:58:01.200 --> 00:58:04.130
Sohonet and Avere for
really being behind us

00:58:04.130 --> 00:58:06.680
for their availability
and their ongoing support,

00:58:06.680 --> 00:58:08.060
and for the GCP team.

00:58:08.060 --> 00:58:11.000
I mean, Thomas, [INAUDIBLE],
[INAUDIBLE], and Jeff,

00:58:11.000 --> 00:58:12.170
these guys are amazing.

00:58:12.170 --> 00:58:14.264
And just this ongoing
collaboration,

00:58:14.264 --> 00:58:16.430
we're looking forward to
see what the future brings.

00:58:16.430 --> 00:58:19.700
So with this, just want to
thank you all for listening.

00:58:19.700 --> 00:58:20.270
And I guess--

00:58:20.270 --> 00:58:22.670
[APPLAUSE]

00:58:23.270 --> 00:58:26.620
[MUSIC PLAYING]

